  "assessment": "All sixty-three exact-target rows read: thirteen direct and three stable generators, thirty support topics, six examples and eleven conditional outcomes. Closest owns fourteen generators including Planning, while Human Geography and Global Urbanism/Transnational Planning independently ground the two candidate directions.",
  "sourceSupport": {
    "20436–20451": "Public programme and actual current branch/course substance corroborate people/place, institutions, inequality, housing/mobility/environment and planning futures. Formal three-track table resolves stale introductory prose. Dedicated housing/mobility depth is acknowledged as a UCR gap rather than falsely supplied by broad courses.",
    "20452–20481": "Current geographic, planning, GIS, methods, field and thesis content substantiate supporting topics. Some are options across the broader degree, not all extra compulsory Planning units.",
    "20482–20498": "Six examples and eleven potential research/postgraduate/professional outcomes remain search-only and conditional. No automatic planner qualification, employment or admissions entitlement follows."
  },
  "candidateReassessment": {
    "closest": "Coherent people/place/environmental evidence plus governance/policy and social research. Replaced materials/recycling laboratories with actual public economics and policy analysis. The environmental foundation remains useful for the explicit GIS, climate and delta-water core, without mislabelling laboratory science as urban planning.",
    "public": "Distinct institutional/distributive purposes with public economics replacing consumer product design. Legal/property/institutional and psychological inclusion/communication roles are bounded; no tenancy specialisation or planning studio is fabricated.",
    "global": "Actual global, imperial, Indigenous, racial and heritage connections materially respond to the human-geography direction. Narrowed title to Global connections, identity & place and removed sustained migration/youth-extremism-training claims. Replaced educational extremism course with social psychology for identity/group processes. Other area-specific examples are explicit rather than portrayed as general global-geography teaching."
  }
